WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
The roasting line of Chapters 14 to 16: 3,400,000 out, 1,050,000 a year for five years, 550,000 more at the end; a net present value of 1,041,630 at 9 per cent; an internal rate of return of about 19.5 per cent. Chapter 16 also listed net present value's two blind spots — it says nothing about the speed of recovery and nothing about value relative to the amount invested. This chapter fills both.
17.1  The payback period
The payback period is the time taken for a project's cash inflows to recover its initial investment. It is not a measure of profitability and it was never meant to be: it is a measure of exposure, answering how long the company's money is at risk.
Where the inflows are level, divide. Where they are uneven, accumulate them year by year until the investment is covered, then take the fraction of the final year needed.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: the roasting line's payback
Step 1. Level flows, so divide. The investment is 3,400,000 and the annual inflow 1,050,000, so the payback is 3,400,000 ÷ 1,050,000 = 3.24 years.
Step 2. The long way, which also handles uneven flows. Three years bring in 1,050,000 × 3 = 3,150,000, leaving 3,400,000 − 3,150,000 = 250,000 to recover.
Step 3. Take the fraction. That remainder is 250,000 ÷ 1,050,000 = 0.238 of the fourth year's inflow, so the payback is 3 + 0.24 = 3.24 years, agreeing with step 1.
Check. Note what step 3 assumes: that cash arrives evenly through the fourth year. It does not, for a business with a harvest in it, and a payback stated to two decimals from annual data is as falsely precise as anything Chapter 16 warned about. "Between three and four years" is the honest answer.
Its advantages, and they are real.
It is simple and universally understood. A plant manager who will never discount anything understands three and a quarter years.
It measures liquidity exposure, which matters to a company that cannot afford to have its cash tied up — Book 1's Chapter 11 and Book 2's whole working-capital section.
It is a crude proxy for risk, because distant cash flows are the least certain, so a short payback means the case rests on the years that can be forecast.
It is useful as a screen. Where a company sees two hundred proposals a year, rejecting those that cannot repay within five is a cheap first filter before the discounting work begins.
Its limitations, which are the examined half.
It ignores everything after the payback point. A project repaying in three years and then stopping ranks equally with one repaying in three years and running for twenty.
It ignores the time value of money within the payback period, treating a dollar in year three as equal to one today — which section 17.2 repairs.
It has no decision rule of its own. The required period is chosen, not derived: nothing in the arithmetic says whether four years is acceptable.
It is biased against exactly the projects that create most value — long-lived infrastructure, research, brand building — because their returns arrive late.
And it is not a profitability measure at all, so a project can have an attractive payback and a negative net present value.
Figure F717-02 sets the two sides out.
Figure F717-02. Payback: what it does and does not do
@table
Question | Payback's answer | Adequate?
How long is our money at risk? | 3.24 years | Yes — this is its job
Does the project create value? | No answer | No
What happens after year 3.24? | Ignored | No
Is a dollar in year 3 worth one today? | Assumed yes | No
Is 3.24 years acceptable? | No answer | No — the standard is set by policy
@end
