17.2  Discounted payback
Discounted payback asks the same question of discounted flows: how long until the present value of the inflows covers the investment? It repairs one of payback's two great faults — the ignoring of time value within the period — and leaves the other untouched, because flows after the recovery point are still ignored.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: the roasting line's discounted payback
Step 1. Discount each year at 9 per cent. The factors are 0.9174, 0.8417, 0.7722, 0.7084 and 0.6499, so the five inflows of 1,050,000 are worth 963,270, 883,785, 810,810, 743,820 and 682,395 respectively.
Step 2. Accumulate. After one year 963,270; after two, 963,270 + 883,785 = 1,847,055; after three, 1,847,055 + 810,810 = 2,657,865.
Step 3. Find the shortfall. The investment is still short by 3,400,000 − 2,657,865 = 742,135 at the end of year three.
Step 4. Take the fraction of year four. That shortfall is 742,135 ÷ 743,820 = 0.998 of the fourth year's discounted inflow, so the discounted payback is 3 + 1.00 = 4.00 years.
Check. Undiscounted 3.24 years; discounted 4.00 years. The difference of three quarters of a year is the cost of the money during the recovery period, and it is always a lengthening: discounting can only make the inflows smaller. A company with a four-year cut-off would accept this project on payback and reject it on discounted payback, which is why the policy must say which one it means.
Two further observations.
A project whose discounted payback is shorter than its life has a positive net present value, necessarily, because by the time the discounted inflows cover the investment the net present value has passed zero, and every later flow adds to it. So discounted payback at least cannot contradict net present value about acceptance, which plain payback can.
And it is harder to explain than payback while being easier to misread: "we get our money back in four years" is not what it says. It says the inflows cover the investment and its financing cost in four years.
