17.3  The profitability index
The profitability index is the present value of a project's inflows divided by the investment. It converts a net present value into a measure of value for each dollar committed, which is precisely the blind spot Chapter 16 identified.
The index for the roasting line is 4,441,630 ÷ 3,400,000 = 1.306, or 1.31 to two decimals: every dollar invested comes back as about $1.31 of present value.
Two conventions exist and both appear in exams. The common one divides the present value of the inflows by the investment, so the accept rule is an index above 1.00. The other divides the net present value by the investment, so the accept rule is an index above zero; the roasting line's index on that basis is 1,041,630 ÷ 3,400,000 = 0.306. The two carry the same information and differ by exactly 1.00. Read the question: if it offers answers near 1.3, it wants the first; near 0.3, the second.
Three things make the index worth having.
It is consistent with net present value about acceptance. An index above 1.00 and a positive net present value are the same statement, because the index exceeds one precisely when the inflows' present value exceeds the outlay.
It ranks correctly under capital rationing, which is section 17.4.
And it states value per dollar, which makes projects of very different sizes comparable in a way net present value cannot.
Its limitation is the mirror image of net present value's. Being a ratio, it ignores absolute size: an index of 1.50 on 200,000 creates 100,000 of value and an index of 1.10 on 10,000,000 creates 1,000,000. Where money is not short, take the bigger value; the index matters only when the money is.
Figure F717-03 compares the three measures on the roasting line.
Figure F717-03. Four readings of one project
@table
Measure | Figure | What it says | Decision
Net present value | 1,041,630 | Value created in money | Accept
Internal rate of return | 19.5 per cent | The project's own return | Accept
Profitability index | 1.31 | Present value per dollar invested | Accept
Discounted payback | 4.00 years | Years to recover investment and its cost | Policy decides
@end
