17.6  The accounting rate of return, and choosing among the measures
The accounting rate of return — also met as the return on investment of Book 1 and as the simple or unadjusted rate of return — divides average annual accounting profit by the investment. It discounts nothing and uses profit rather than cash, which makes it the weakest of the measures and the one most often seen first, because it is the only one that speaks the language of the financial statements the project will eventually appear in.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: the roasting line's accounting rate of return
Step 1. From cash flow to profit. The line's pre-tax operating cash flow is 1,200,000 a year, and depreciation of 1,200,000 − 600,000 = 600,000 must be deducted to reach accounting profit, giving 600,000 before tax.
Step 2. After tax. At 25 per cent, 600,000 × 0.75 = 450,000 of average annual profit.
Step 3. On the initial investment. 450,000 ÷ 3,400,000 = 13.24 per cent.
Step 4. On the average investment. Where the convention is to use the average of the opening and closing book amounts, (3,400,000 + 550,000) ÷ 2 = 1,975,000, so the rate is 450,000 ÷ 1,975,000 = 22.78 per cent.
Check. The same project is 13.24 per cent or 22.78 per cent depending on a convention nobody outside the finance department knows about, and neither figure can be compared with the 9 per cent cost of capital, because one is an accounting ratio and the other a required return on cash. That is the measure's central weakness and the reason a question that offers both figures is testing whether you read the word average.
Its weaknesses, stated plainly for the exam: it ignores the time value of money entirely; it uses accrual profit rather than cash, so it is affected by the depreciation method and by every accrual Book 1's Chapter 17 discussed; it depends on an arbitrary choice between initial and average investment; and it has no principled accept rule. Its one genuine advantage is that it is the measure in which the project's effect on reported results and on the divisional performance measures of Book 4 will actually show up — which matters for the behaviour of the manager being asked to propose it.
Which measure, then. The practical answer used by most well-run companies is to compute several and let each answer its own question. Net present value decides. The internal rate of return communicates and gives the margin over the hurdle. The profitability index ranks under rationing. Payback or discounted payback states the liquidity exposure against a policy limit. The accounting rate of return shows what the financial statements will say. A proposal carrying all five, with the sensitivity analysis of Chapter 15 beside them, is a proposal a board can actually decide on.
YOUR TURN   Five measures, one project
