A.	discounts the inflows before accumulating them.
B.	includes flows after recovery.
C.	uses accounting profit.
D.	ignores the investment.
P17-04  Cumulative present value is 2,657,865 after three years and the fourth year's discounted inflow is 743,820. On an outlay of 3,400,000 the discounted payback is about:
A.	4.0 years
B.	3.2 years
C.	3.5 years
D.	4.6 years
P17-05  The profitability index is:
A.	the present value of inflows divided by the investment.
B.	net present value divided by the inflows.
C.	the investment divided by annual cash flow.
D.	profit divided by sales.
P17-06  An index of 1.31 means each dollar invested returns:
A.	about $1.31 of present value.
B.	31 cents of cash a year.
C.	$1.31 of profit.
D.	131 per cent a year.
P17-07  Under a fixed capital budget, ranking by net present value rather than by profitability index tends to favour:
A.	larger projects over more productive ones.
B.	shorter projects.
C.	divisible projects.
D.	projects with earlier flows.
P17-08  Hard capital rationing arises from:
A.	an inability to raise further money on acceptable terms.
B.	a board-imposed budget.
C.	a desire to filter weak proposals.
D.	distrust of forecasts.
P17-09  With a budget of 5,000,000, Project A needs 5,000,000 for 900,000 of value while B and C each need 2,500,000 for 600,000 and 550,000. The best total value is:
A.	1,150,000
B.	900,000
C.	2,050,000
D.	550,000
P17-10  Average annual profit of 450,000 on an average investment of 1,975,000 is an accounting rate of return of:
A.	22.78 per cent
B.	13.24 per cent
C.	43.9 per cent
D.	9.00 per cent
P17-11  Which measure ignores the time value of money entirely?
A.	The accounting rate of return
B.	Discounted payback
C.	The profitability index
D.	Net present value
P17-12  A project with a short payback and a negative net present value should be:
A.	rejected, because payback is not a profitability measure.
B.	accepted, because the money returns quickly.
C.	accepted if the payback is under three years.
D.	ranked by its accounting rate of return.
Case-style practice: the capital budget
Orontes's board has set next year's capital budget at $4,000,000 — soft rationing, imposed because last year's forecasts proved optimistic. Four proposals compete, all with positive net present values at 9 per cent.
@table
Proposal | Investment | Present value of inflows | Net present value
Roasting line | 3,400,000 | 4,441,630 | 1,041,630
Warehouse racking | 1,600,000 | 2,000,000 | 400,000
Boiler replacement | 2,400,000 | 2,880,000 | 480,000
Fleet renewal | 600,000 | 810,000 | 210,000
@end
None can be taken in part. Required. Compute each profitability index, recommend a combination, and comment on the board's decision to impose the limit. Under 350 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC17-1  Answer A. It is a duration, not a return.
B is wrong. It measures no profit.
C is wrong. Value is net present value's job.
D is wrong. That is a separate measure.
SC17-2  Answer A. 3,400,000 ÷ 1,050,000 = 3.24 years.
B is wrong. Three years recover only 3,150,000.
C is wrong. That is the discounted figure.
D is wrong. This has no basis in the data.
SC17-3  Answer A. Everything after recovery is ignored.
B is wrong. It is the easiest measure to compute.
C is wrong. Plain payback uses no rate.
D is wrong. Uneven flows are handled by accumulation.
SC17-4  Answer A. Discounting shrinks the inflows, so recovery takes longer.
B is wrong. It can never be shorter.
C is wrong. Only at a zero discount rate.
D is wrong. There is no such relationship.
SC17-5  Answer A. Recovery inside the life means value has passed zero.
B is wrong. The opposite follows.
C is wrong. A positive value means the rate beats the hurdle.
D is wrong. The index would exceed one.
SC17-6  Answer A. Later flows are still excluded.
B is wrong. Time value is exactly what it adds.
C is wrong. The investment is the target to be recovered.
D is wrong. The rate is used in the discounting.
SC17-7  Answer A. 4,441,630 ÷ 3,400,000 = 1.306, or 1.31.
B is wrong. That inverts the ratio.
C is wrong. This has no basis in the figures.
D is wrong. That is the payback period.
SC17-8  Answer A. Inflows must exceed the outlay.
B is wrong. Zero is the rule on the net-present-value convention.
C is wrong. The index is not a rate.
D is wrong. That is this project's index, not the rule.
SC17-9  Answer A. A ratio says nothing about magnitude.
B is wrong. Time value is in the present values.
C is wrong. Any flows can be discounted.
D is wrong. The two always agree about acceptance.
SC17-10  Answer A. The reversal point for each input, one at a time.
B is wrong. Probabilities come from simulation.
C is wrong. Correlations are what scenarios capture.
D is wrong. The rate is an input to be tested, not an output.
SC17-11  Answer A. 1,041,630 ÷ 3,400,000 = 30.64 per cent.
B is wrong. That is the discount rate.
C is wrong. That is the internal rate of return.
D is wrong. That is the margin on the annual cash flow.
SC17-12  Answer A. Value per unit of the constraint.
B is wrong. That rewards size under a budget.
C is wrong. Payback measures no value.
D is wrong. It discounts nothing.
SC17-13  Answer A. Soft rationing is self-imposed.
B is wrong. That is hard rationing.
C is wrong. Covenants are external.
D is wrong. Market conditions are external.
SC17-14  Answer A. Divisibility makes the ranking exact.
B is wrong. Mutual exclusivity is a different constraint.
C is wrong. Equal size would remove the problem.
D is wrong. Life is not the issue.
SC17-15  Answer A. No discounting, and profit rather than cash.
B is wrong. It is simple to compute.
C is wrong. It uses none.
D is wrong. It can be compared; the comparison is just unsound.
SC17-16  Answer A. 450,000 ÷ 3,400,000 = 13.24 per cent.
B is wrong. That uses the average investment.
C is wrong. This has no basis in the figures.
D is wrong. That is the internal rate of return.
Your turn
@table
Item | Answer
Payback period | 3.24 years: 3,400,000 ÷ 1,050,000 = 3.24.
Discounted payback | 4.00 years: the shortfall of 742,135 is almost exactly the fourth year's discounted inflow.
Profitability index | 1.31: 4,441,630 ÷ 3,400,000 = 1.306.
Net present value | 1,041,630: 4,441,630 − 3,400,000 = 1,041,630.
Measure that ranks under a fixed budget | The profitability index, which maximises value per dollar of the constraint.
Measure the financial statements will show | The accounting rate of return, which uses accrual profit.
What it tells you | Five measures, one project, one decision — accept. The measures differ in what they say when money is short or when liquidity is the worry, which is exactly when more than one is needed.
@end
Multiple-choice questions
P17-01  Answer A. A duration.
B is wrong. No value is measured.
C is wrong. No return is measured.
D is wrong. Plain payback uses no rate.
P17-02  Answer A. 3,400,000 ÷ 1,050,000 = 3.24 years.
B is wrong. Three years are not enough.
C is wrong. This inverts the figures.
D is wrong. That is the discounted payback.
P17-03  Answer A. The inflows are discounted first.
B is wrong. Both ignore later flows.
C is wrong. Neither uses profit.
D is wrong. Both target the investment.
P17-04  Answer A. The shortfall of 742,135 is nearly all of 743,820.
B is wrong. That is the undiscounted payback.
C is wrong. Recovery is not complete by then.
D is wrong. The fourth year completes it.
P17-05  Answer A. Present value of inflows over investment.
B is wrong. That ratio is not used.
C is wrong. That is the payback period.
D is wrong. That is a margin.
P17-06  Answer A. $1.31 of present value for each dollar.
B is wrong. The index is not an annual figure.
C is wrong. It is present value, not profit.
D is wrong. It is not a rate.
P17-07  Answer A. Size wins over productivity under a constraint.
B is wrong. Length is not what the measures differ on here.
C is wrong. Divisibility is an assumption, not a bias.
D is wrong. Timing is handled by discounting in both.
P17-08  Answer A. Inability to raise money is external.
B is wrong. A board limit is soft rationing.
C is wrong. Filtering is a reason for soft rationing.
D is wrong. Distrust of forecasts is also internal.
P17-09  Answer A. 600,000 + 550,000 = 1,150,000 from B and C.
B is wrong. That is A alone, the greedy answer.
C is wrong. The budget cannot fund all three.
D is wrong. That is C alone.
P17-10  Answer A. 450,000 ÷ 1,975,000 = 22.78 per cent.
B is wrong. That uses the initial investment.
C is wrong. This has no basis in the figures.
D is wrong. That is the cost of capital.
P17-11  Answer A. The accounting rate of return discounts nothing.
B is wrong. Discounting is in its name.
C is wrong. Its numerator is a present value.
D is wrong. Discounting is its method.
P17-12  Answer A. Payback says nothing about value.
B is wrong. Quick recovery of a value-destroying project is still value destroyed.
C is wrong. No cut-off makes a negative value acceptable.
D is wrong. That measure is weaker still.
Case-style set: the capital budget
The indices. Roasting line: 4,441,630 ÷ 3,400,000 = 1.31. Racking: 2,000,000 ÷ 1,600,000 = 1.25. Boiler: 2,880,000 ÷ 2,400,000 = 1.20. Fleet: 810,000 ÷ 600,000 = 1.35.
The index ranking is therefore fleet, roasting line, racking, boiler.
Testing the combinations. The projects are indivisible, so the ranking is a starting point rather than the answer. Taking the fleet at 600,000 leaves 3,400,000, which the roasting line uses exactly: total value 1,041,630 + 210,000 = 1,251,630, and the budget is fully spent. The alternatives are worse: racking plus boiler costs 4,000,000 for 400,000 + 480,000 = 880,000; fleet plus racking plus... the boiler will not fit alongside both. No feasible combination beats 1,251,630.
Recommendation: the fleet renewal and the roasting line, for a total of 1,251,630 of value on exactly 4,000,000 of capital.
On the limit itself. The board is turning down the racking and the boiler, which together would create another 880,000 of value, so the self-imposed budget costs the company that much on these figures. Three things should be said about it. First, the reason given — optimistic forecasts — is a reason to correct the forecasts, not to cap the spending: capping punishes the honest proposals along with the hopeful ones. Second, if the concern is specific projects, the postaudit evidence of Chapter 15 should identify which sponsors and which kinds of project were optimistic, and the 880,000 may be partly illusory. Third, if the limit is really about something else — debt capacity, covenants, the organisation's ability to absorb four projects at once — that should be said plainly, because it is a better reason and it points to a different answer: stage the racking and the boiler into next year rather than reject them.
